<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empress Dowager Cixi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cixi · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Empress Dowager Cixi was the dominant figure at the Qing court from the 1860s until her death in 1908, ruling as regent for the Tongzhi and Guangxu emperors. She supported some limited modernisation but suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> of 1898 and backed the Boxers in 1900. Her rule is often associated with the late decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi.jpg" alt="Empress Dowager Cixi" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Yu Shuinling · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ci-Xi_Imperial_Dowager_Empress_(9.2).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b>’s suppression of the Hundred Days’ Reform in 1898, placing the reformist Guangxu Emperor under house arrest and executing six of his reformist allies, ended the most ambitious internal attempt at systemic renewal, and the disastrous Boxer Uprising of 1900 brought foreign occupation of Beijing…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Xianfeng Emperor died at Rehe in 1861, a palace coup gave power to his widow, the Empress Dowager Ci’an, and to <b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b>, mother of the five-year-old Tongzhi Emperor, who ruled together with Prince Gong.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Zaitian, a nephew of <b>Cixi</b>, took the throne aged three after the Tongzhi Emperor died without an heir, adopting the regnal name Guangxu, &quot;continuation of splendor&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing dynasty's European-trained Ever Victorious Army defeated Taiping forces at <b>Cixi</b>, but its commander, the American mercenary Frederick Townsend Ward, was mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Imperial City. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1882" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Empress Dowager Cixi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
